###  http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#TargetCommunity
:TargetCommunity rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf :Dimension ;
                 :dimensionIndex 11 ;
                 :promptExtractionText "Extract Target Community elements from the image. Target Community includes: audience indicators, community markers, demographic cues, cultural references, community-specific elements, and target audience signals. For each community element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'internet_audience', 'meme_culture'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the internet audience', 'the meme culture'), 3) A brief description of the intended audience or community. Focus on elements that indicate who the meme is designed for. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"internet_audience\", \"label\": \"the internet audience\", \"description\": \"The meme appears designed for internet-savvy audiences familiar with meme culture and dark humor\"}"@en ;
                 rdfs:comment "The intended audience or community for which the meme is designed"@en ;
                 rdfs:label "Target Community"@en .


###  http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#TemplateStructure
:TemplateStructure rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf :Dimension ;
                   :dimensionIndex 12 ;
                   :promptExtractionText "Extract Template Structure elements from the image. Template Structure includes: template patterns, organizational frameworks, structural elements, layout templates, framework components, and structural patterns. For each structural element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'contrast_template', 'layout_framework'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the contrast template', 'the layout framework'), 3) A brief description of the structural pattern or framework. Focus on the underlying organizational structure of the meme. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"contrast_template\", \"label\": \"the contrast template\", \"description\": \"The meme follows a contrast template with calm foreground subject against chaotic background scene\"}"@en ;
                   rdfs:comment "Underlying structural patterns and organizational frameworks of memes"@en ;
                   rdfs:label "Template Structure"@en .


###  http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#TemporalRelation
:TemporalRelation rdf:type owl:Class ;
                  rdfs:subClassOf :DimensionsInterrelation ;
                  rdfs:comment "One dimension being temporally related with another in memes, e.g. text adding before/after context to visual material"@en ;
                  rdfs:label "Temporal Relation"@en .


###  http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#TextualMaterial
:TextualMaterial rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf :Dimension ;
                 :dimensionIndex 2 ;
                 :promptExtractionText "Extract Textual Material elements from the image. Textual Material includes: captions, text overlays, speech bubbles, written words, phrases, sentences, and any visible text. For each textual element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'main_caption', 'speech_bubble_text'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the main caption', 'the speech bubble text'), 3) A brief description of the text content and its context. Focus on all visible text elements that contribute to the meme's meaning. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"main_caption\", \"label\": \"the main caption\", \"description\": \"The text 'This is fine' written in bold letters at the bottom of the image\"}"@en ;
                 :prototypicalQuestions """1. What written text appears in this meme? (Include all visible words, phrases, captions, and labels)

2. How is the text positioned and formatted within the meme? (Consider placement, font size, color, style, and visual hierarchy)

3. What is the tone and style of the written language used? (Formal/informal, serious/humorous, slang/standard, etc.)

4. Are there any spelling, grammar, or punctuation choices that seem intentional? (Consider deliberate errors, unconventional capitalization, or punctuation for effect)

5. What cultural references, phrases, or terminology are embedded in the text? (Identify idioms, pop culture references, internet slang, or specialized vocabulary)

6. How does the text relate to or interact with the visual elements? (Does it complement, contradict, or add context to the images?)

7. What assumptions does the text make about the audience's knowledge? (What background information or cultural literacy is assumed?)

8. Are there multiple layers of meaning in the textual content? (Consider literal vs. implied meanings, wordplay, double entendres, or irony)

9. What speech acts or communicative functions does the text perform? (Does it inform, persuade, mock, celebrate, complain, etc.?)

10. How does the text contribute to the meme's overall message or humor? (What role does the written content play in achieving the meme's intended effect?)""" ;
                 rdfs:comment "Textual Material includes: captions, text overlays, speech bubbles, written words, phrases, sentences, and any visible text."@en ;
                 rdfs:label "Textual Material"@en .


###  http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general#Toxicity
:Toxicity rdf:type owl:Class ;
                    rdfs:subClassOf :Dimension ;
                    :dimensionIndex 13 ;
                    :promptExtractionText "Extract Toxicity Assessment elements from the image. Toxicity Assessment includes: harmful elements, offensive content, toxic indicators, problematic elements, negative aspects, and harmful signals. For each toxicity element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'dark_humor', 'potential_harm'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the dark humor', 'the potential harm'), 3) A brief description of the potentially harmful or problematic aspect. Focus on elements that might be considered offensive, harmful, or problematic. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"dark_humor\", \"label\": \"the dark humor\", \"description\": \"The meme uses dark humor by showing a smiling girl in front of a burning house, which could be considered insensitive to real tragedy\"}"@en ;
                    rdfs:comment "Toxicity Assessment includes: harmful elements, offensive content, toxic indicators, problematic elements, negative aspects, and harmful signals."@en ;
                    rdfs:label "Toxicity Assessment"@en .


